## 🛫 PILOT — Ticket Pipeline

Feature and technical work moves through **PILOT**, a lightweight alternative to
running the full [BMAD-METHOD](https://github.com/bmad-code-org/BMAD-METHOD) agent
framework: the same idea — specialized agent personas taking a ticket from a raw idea to
a merged PR — at a fraction of the token cost, because each phase runs in its own short,
focused context instead of one big context carrying the whole history. PILOT is provided
by the [`pilot`](https://github.com/Jonas-Eve/pilot) Claude Code plugin, not this repo —
see `.claude/settings.json` for how it's pinned, and `/pilot:update` to pull in the
plugin's process-doc/label updates.

Five phases, each with its own Claude Code skill and agent persona:

| Phase | Skill | Agent | Produces |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1. Plan | `/pilot:story` | PM | A functional user story |
| 2. Investigate | `/pilot:scope` | Architect | Scoped ticket(s) with security/architecture decisions |
| 3. Lay out | `/pilot:spec` | Tech Lead | A technical implementation spec |
| 4. Operate | `/pilot:dev` | Senior Dev | Code + an open pull request |
| 5. Test & validate | `/pilot:review` | PM + Architect + Tech Lead | A consolidated go/no-go GitHub comment |

Purely technical tickets (CI, security, deployment, migrations, optimization) skip
phase 1 and start directly at `/pilot:scope`. There's no single command that runs all
five phases end to end — each phase is driven on its own, one at a time — and each also
runs bare, with no argument at all, which is what lets a scheduled cron Routine drive a
phase on a timer instead of a human typing it each time. `/pilot:story` always runs
paired with a human (no Routine for it); `/pilot:scope`, `/pilot:spec`, and `/pilot:dev`
default to that same pair mode too, so their Routine's prompt adds `--auto` to run
unattended; `/pilot:review` is already automatic and needs no flag.
A GitHub label state machine (`status:backlog` → ... → `in-review` → `approved` →
`done`, plus `priority:`, `type:feature`/`type:tech`, and a `type:epic`/`status:split`
tracking hierarchy for work too big for one story) tracks where a ticket is and stops
multiple agents from picking up the same one — including a `status:changes-requested`
loop back to the dev phase when review finds something that needs an actual code fix, as
opposed to a pure judgment call. Two flags sit orthogonal to that state machine, each
cleared by a human once resolved: `needs-human` is what a phase adds when it hits
something only a person can decide right now; `on-hold` instead pauses a ticket for
something bigger in flight elsewhere (a restructuring, a dependency on other work) with
no immediate decision needed. Agents can propose a merge is ready, but a human always
performs the actual merge.

Each phase is a Claude Code slash command (`/pilot:story "..."`, `/pilot:scope 42`,
`/pilot:dev 42`, ...) — see [docs/pilot-process.md](docs/pilot-process.md) §0 for the
required GitHub labels (created once, not auto-created by any skill) and a full command
quickstart, and the rest of that doc for the state machine and claim protocol in detail.
